    ipbes:hasDescription "BIBL {\"uncited\":[],\"omitted\":[],\"custom\":[]} CSL_BIBLIOGRAPHY(https://www.zotero.org/) DOCUMENT_PREFERENCES <data data-version=\"3\" zotero-version=\"8.0.4\"><session id=\"heU7GaFw\"/><style id=\"http://www.zotero.org/styles/apa\" locale=\"en-GB\" hasBibliography=\"1\" bibliographyStyleHasBeenSet=\"0\"/><prefs><pref name=\"fieldType\" value=\"Field\"/><pref name=\"dontAskDelayCitationUpdates\" value=\"true\"/></prefs></data>(https://www.zotero.org/) --------------------------- 1 Although the United Nations Environment Programme pan-European region comes close to the area covered by the IPBES Regional Assessment for Europe and Central Asia, it is not quite identical (see for an overview of countries belonging to the paan-European region). 2 Cross-Compliance comprises Statutory Management Requirements (SMR), referring to standards in environment, food security and animal welfare, and Good Agricultural and Environmental Conditions (GAEC) referring to soil protection, maintenance of soil organic matter and structure, avoiding the deterioration of habitats and water management (Commission Regulation (EC) No 1122/2009). 3 The three greening requirements are: 1) to cultivate at least two or three different crops in case of arable land exceeding 10 ha or 30 ha, respectively ; 2) to maintain permanent pasture; and 3) to establish ecological focus areas on at least 5% of arable land exceeding 15 ha . 4 “Three types of high nature value farmland are identified: Type 1: Farmland with a high proportion of semi-natural vegetation; Type 2: Farmland with a mosaic of low intensity agriculture and natural and structural elements, such as field margins, hedgerows, stone walls, patches of woodland or scrub, small rivers etc.; Type 3: Farmland supporting rare species or a high proportion of European or world populations” . 5 https://enrd.ec.europa.eu/policy-in-action/policy-framework_en 6 https://enrd.ec.europa.eu/leader-clld_en" ;
